From f402794aaf7a8046a3b03661418e587a0ca8a7d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 12:37:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Use qtpy instead of PySide6

DeepLabCut imports PySide6 directly in every GUI module and pins
`QT_API=pyside6` in deeplabcut/gui/__init__.py. conda-forge ships several Qt
bindings and users routinely already have one installed, so hard-coding one
binding forces a second, redundant Qt stack into the environment and breaks
co-installation with anything that selected a different binding (napari being
the obvious one).

Route every GUI import through qtpy and let it pick the binding, rather than
overriding the user's choice:

* drop the forced QT_API assignment so qtpy's own detection wins;
* rewrite the PySide6 imports in the 28 GUI modules to the qtpy equivalents;
* import Qt from qtpy.QtCore in gui/widgets.py -- PySide6 happens to re-export
  it from QtGui, but that is not portable across bindings;
* ask qdarkstyle for the stylesheet of whichever binding was selected
  (load_stylesheet()) instead of load_stylesheet_pyside6()/_pyside2(). This
  also fixes window.py's darkmode(), which asked for the PySide2 stylesheet
  unconditionally while lightmode() already used the binding-agnostic call;
* probe qtpy rather than PySide6 in __main__.py when deciding whether the GUI
  is available.
